import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { RadioHero } from "../../demos/radio/hero.rx"
import { RadioCards } from "../../demos/radio/cards.rx"
import { RadioSaving } from "../../demos/radio/saving.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Radio" subtitle="A set of native radios of which one is checked.">
    <Demo source="radio/hero"><RadioHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="radio/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      Each <code>RadioRoot</code> is a native <code>{"<input type=\"radio\">"}</code>, and the radios of a group
      share a <code>name</code>. That is all the browser needs to treat them as one group. It provides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>One Tab stop for the group: the checked radio, or the first one when none is checked.</li>
      <li>
        Moving between the radios with the arrow keys, checking each one as focus arrives and skipping disabled
        ones. Chrome and Firefox wrap around at the ends; Safari doesn't.
      </li>
      <li><kbd>Space</kbd> to check the focused radio, and label association.</li>
      <li>Form submission of the checked radio's value, and <code>required</code> validation.</li>
      <li>
        The style hooks <code>:checked</code>, <code>:disabled</code>, <code>:focus-visible</code> and{" "}
        <code>:user-invalid</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The library adds <code>RadioGroup</code>, a <code>{"<div role=\"radiogroup\">"}</code> that holds the value
      (which may be controlled) and gives its radios a shared name when you don't. It also adds{" "}
      <code>readOnly</code>, and the wiring to a <a href="../field/">Field</a>. There is no roving focus in
      JavaScript.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        Radio and RadioGroup are documented together, as one family. As with{" "}
        <a href="../checkbox/">Checkbox</a>, an <code>{"<input>"}</code> can't have children, so{" "}
        <code>RadioRoot</code> renders its children after the radio: <code>RadioIndicator</code> is its next
        sibling, styled with <code>.Radio:checked + .Indicator</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="labelling">Labelling the group</h3>
    <p>
      Name the group with <code>aria-label</code>, or with <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing at a caption as
      the demo above does. Inside a <code>FieldRoot</code>, a <code>FieldLabel</code> names it, and a{" "}
      <a href="../fieldset/">Fieldset</a>'s legend works too. Each radio is labelled by its own{" "}
      <code>{"<label>"}</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> and <code>onValueChange</code> to the group to hold the value yourself, or{" "}
      <code>defaultValue</code> to let it keep its own. An owner that doesn't take the new value keeps its radio
      checked. With no value, no radio is checked and the first one is the group's Tab stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the demo below the whole card is the radio's <code>{"<label>"}</code>, and is styled from the radio inside
      it with <code>:has(:checked)</code> and <code>:has(:focus-visible)</code>. The radio draws its own dot, with
      an inset shadow, so there is no indicator.
    </p>
    <Demo source="radio/cards"><RadioCards /></Demo>

    <h3 id="names">Names and forms</h3>
    <p>
      The radios submit the checked value under the group's <code>name</code>. Without one, the field's name is
      used inside a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, and otherwise a generated name groups them. A form they are in
      then submits the value under that generated name, so give the group a <code>name</code> when the submission
      matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The radio holding the group's <code>defaultValue</code> carries the <code>checked</code> attribute, which is
      what the browser checks again when the form is reset. An uncontrolled group then takes its default as its
      value and calls <code>onValueChange</code> once if it changed (a group with no default is cleared without a
      call, as <code>onValueChange</code> receives a value). A controlled group is set back to its owner's value.
    </p>

    <h3 id="disabled-read-only">Disabled and read-only</h3>
    <p>
      <code>disabled</code> on a radio, or on the group, is the native attribute: the arrow keys skip a disabled
      radio and <code>:disabled</code> matches it. <code>readOnly</code> on the group lets focus move through the
      radios with the arrow keys but checks none of them; the group carries <code>aria-readonly</code> and each
      radio <code>data-readonly</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="saving">Saving to a server</h3>
    <p>
      A setting that saves when it changes: <code>onValueChange</code> sends the new choice, and the group shows
      its owner's <code>value</code>. Write the handler as an action with a <code>guess</code>, and the new choice
      shows at once, <code>pending?</code> says it is saving, and it goes back by itself if the save fails. Ending a
      save with <code>refresh</code> asks the server what it holds, so when the group is changed again while a
      save is out, the last choice is what stays, whatever order the answers come back in.
    </p>
    <Demo source="radio/saving"><RadioSaving /></Demo>
    <p>
      The group calls <code>onValueChange</code> in a change of its own, so a handler that waits for a server never
      holds it: it can be changed again at once. Without a guess, the group shows the owner's choice until the
      owner writes the new one: the browser's change is put back right after the press.
    </p>
    <p>
      To stop changes while a save is out, pass <code>readOnly={"{"}pending? setting{"}"}</code>: focus stays where it
      is and presses are ignored. Disabled the same way, the focused radio loses focus, as every disabled control
      does, and takes it back when it is enabled again if focus hasn't moved elsewhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>value</code> read from an answer that fails doesn't stop the group: the failure is the app's to
      show, in an <code>errored</code> region around it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="radio" />
  </DocsPage>
}
